    "reading": {
        "body": "New Mexico's 2026 legislative session produced six AI-specific bills and none of them address data center power demand.\n\nThe AI bills cover accountability, government use, transparency, chatbot safety, likeness rights, and AI ethics as a school elective. The rest of the 36-bill list is dominated by interstate health licensure compacts and appropriations, which is the ordinary business of a small legislature. One bill, SB36, offers a tax credit for quantum facility infrastructure, which is adjacent to compute but not the same thing.\n\nThe only grid signal in the record is a news item about Senator Heinrich's GRID Savings Act, a federal proposal to make AI data centers pay for grid upgrades. That is federal, not New Mexico law, and it appears here as coverage rather than as state action. A separate item notes bipartisan opposition to AI data centers, with one additional outlet carrying it.\n\nWhat this does not show is whether New Mexico has data centers, how much power they draw, or what the state's utilities have asked for. The bill titles alone do not reveal whether any AI measure passed, was amended, or died. The absence of grid legislation in a state that is debating data centers is the pattern worth noting, but the data cannot say whether that absence is a choice or a gap.",
